## 🚀 Quick Start: Honeypot Setup

Get real attack data flowing through Flink Agents in **5 minutes**:

### Step 0: Install the CLI

```bash
pip install -e .
# or: pip install -r requirements.txt && python3 -m flink_cowrie --help
```

### Step 1: Build and Start Services

```bash
# Build the Flink Agents Docker image (uses Flink Agents v0.3-SNAPSHOT / main by default)
flink-cowrie build

# Or build with a specific git ref:
# flink-cowrie build release-0.2.1  # Latest stable tagged release
# flink-cowrie build 0.3-SNAPSHOT   # Same as default (main)

# Start Cowrie honeypot + Flink Agents + Dashboard
flink-cowrie up
# equivalent: flink-cowrie up --profile full
```

### CLI reference

| Command | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| `flink-cowrie build [version]` | Build Docker image |
| `flink-cowrie up [--profile full\|minimal]` | Start stack |
| `flink-cowrie down` | Stop stack |
| `flink-cowrie status` | Show running services |
| `flink-cowrie logs [service] -f` | Tail logs |
| `flink-cowrie demo <name>` | Run demo in TaskManager |
| `flink-cowrie dashboard` | Launch Streamlit UI |
| `flink-cowrie sync` | Hot-sync files into container |
| `flink-cowrie process logs` | Run log processor locally |
| `flink-cowrie config show` | Show configuration |
| `flink-cowrie utils simulate-attacks` | Simulate honeypot attacks |
| `flink-cowrie test launch [--build]` | Smoke-test Flink Agents (local runner) |
| `flink-cowrie test launch --cluster --in-container` | Also submit `flink-cowrie-cluster-launch-test` via `flink run` (shows on Web UI) |
| `flink-cowrie test phase1 [--e2e]` | Submit Phase 1 Kafka normalize job via `flink run` (shows under **Running Jobs**) |
| `flink-cowrie test actor-classify [--e2e]` | Phase 1.5 actor classification (timing + injection traps) |
| `flink-cowrie test production --e2e` | Verify Phase 2 + Phase 3 topics from one event |
| `flink-cowrie test react --compare` | Side-by-side workflow vs ReAct (CLI) |

This starts:
- **Cowrie Honeypot** on ports 2222 (SSH) and 2223 (Telnet) - captures real attacks
- **Flink Agents** processing pipeline - analyzes attacks in real-time
- **Threat Detection Dashboard** on port 8501 - visualize threats
- **Flink Web UI** on port 8081 - monitor processing
- **Kafka (optional)** on port 9092 - can be used as a streaming dashboard source (`cowrie.alerts`)

### Step 2: View the Dashboard

Open your browser:

- **Threat Detection Dashboard**: http://localhost:8501
- **Flink Web UI**: http://localhost:8081
- **Flink completed jobs** (after cluster launch test): http://localhost:8081/#/job/completed

In the Streamlit sidebar, choose **Data source**:
- **JSON file**: reads `cowrie-dashboard-data.json` (default quickstart)
- **Kafka topic**: reads `cowrie.alerts` from Kafka
- **Both**: merges file + Kafka and dedupes by `alert_id`

![Honeypot Dashboard](images/HoneypotDashboard.png)

*Real-time threat detection dashboard showing attacks, alerts, and response actions*

### Step 2b: Verify Flink cluster jobs (optional)

To confirm Flink Agents can run on the **compose cluster** (not just the local runner):

```bash
flink-cowrie up --profile minimal
flink-cowrie test launch --cluster --in-container
```

On success, the test prints a job ID and link. In the Flink Web UI, open **Completed Jobs** and find **`flink-cowrie-cluster-launch-test`** in **FINISHED** state. See [test/README.md](test/README.md) for architecture and troubleshooting.

**Phase 1 (Kafka normalize)** on the full stack:

```bash
flink-cowrie up --profile full
flink-cowrie test phase1          # submit + wait for RUNNING
flink-cowrie test phase1 --e2e    # also verify cowrie.normalized output
```

The normalize job appears under **Running Jobs** as **`Cowrie Phase1 Normalize (Kafka)`**. The compose `kafka-normalizer` service submits the same job on startup (set `PHASE1_PYTHON_NORMALIZER=1` for the legacy Python-only sidecar).

**Phase 2 (workflow alerts)** on the full stack:

```bash
flink-cowrie test phase2          # submit + wait for RUNNING
flink-cowrie test phase2 --e2e    # also verify cowrie.alerts output
```

The workflow job appears as **`Cowrie Phase2 Workflow (Kafka)`** under **Running Jobs**. Set `PHASE2_PYTHON_PROCESSOR=1` on `kafka-workflow-processor` for the legacy Python-only sidecar. Production default is **`COWRIE_PHASE2_ENGINE=pure_python`**; set `COWRIE_PHASE2_ENGINE=flink_agents` to try the Flink Agents `from_datastream` spike (see [docs/SPRINT_ROADMAP.md](docs/SPRINT_ROADMAP.md)).

**Legacy (deprecated):** the old single-job path `cowrie_kafka_flink_job.py` is opt-in only — `docker compose --profile legacy up -d kafka-flink-job`. Default compose uses Phase 1 + Phase 2 instead.

**Phase 1.5 (actor classification)** on the full stack:

```bash
flink-cowrie test actor-classify          # unit + session smoke tests
flink-cowrie test actor-classify --e2e    # also verify in JobManager container
```

Phase 1.5 runs as compose service **`kafka-actor-classifier`** (Flink job on `:8081` as **`Cowrie Phase1.5 Actor Classify (Kafka)`**). It consumes **`cowrie.normalized`**, scores sessions (timing + prompt-injection compliance), and writes **`cowrie.normalized.enriched`** plus **`cowrie.session_actor`**. Phase 2/3 consume the enriched topic when **`COWRIE_ACTOR_CLASSIFY=1`**. See [ACTOR_CLASSIFICATION.md](docs/ACTOR_CLASSIFICATION.md).

**Phase 3 (Cloudera ReAct augmentor)** on the full stack:

```bash
flink-cowrie test phase3          # smoke (imports, schema, Cloudera config check)
flink-cowrie test phase3 --e2e    # ReAct LLM on sample event + Kafka verify
```

Phase 3 runs as compose service **`kafka-react-augmentor`** (Kafka sidecar — not visible on `:8081`). It consumes **`cowrie.normalized`**, runs Cloudera ReAct, and writes **`cowrie.react_alerts`** plus REACT-* dashboard rows. Requires **`CLOUDERA_AI_BASE_URL`** and **`CLOUDERA_JWT_TOKEN`** in `.env`.

Optional **Sprint C1 summary agent** (lighter single LLM step for CRITICAL events): set **`COWRIE_PHASE3_SUMMARY=1`** on `kafka-react-augmentor`. Default `0` keeps full ReAct on all events.

Optional **Sprint C2 MCP threat intel** (AbuseIPDB for ReAct `check_ip_reputation`): set **`COWRIE_MCP_THREAT_INTEL=1`** and **`ABUSEIPDB_API_KEY`**. Default `0` uses offline mock reputation. See `.env.example`.

**Production split** (default in `docker-compose-cowrie.yml`):

| Path | Topic | Engine |
|------|-------|--------|
| Phase 2 workflow | `cowrie.alerts` | `pure_python` (default) or `flink_agents` spike (`COWRIE_PHASE2_ENGINE`) |
| Phase 3 ReAct | `cowrie.react_alerts` | Cloudera LLM (`cloudera_react` or `cloudera_summary` when summary flag on) |
| log-processor | dashboard JSON only | **Workflow on hot path** (`COWRIE_HOT_PATH_ENGINE=workflow`); ReAct async in Phase 3 |

See [docs/PRODUCTION_ARCHITECTURE.md](docs/PRODUCTION_ARCHITECTURE.md) for hot-path vs enrichment, dashboard scope, and env vars. Sprint status: [docs/SPRINT_ROADMAP.md](docs/SPRINT_ROADMAP.md).

```bash
flink-cowrie test production          # smoke (topic routing + hot-path policy)
flink-cowrie test production --e2e   # verify both topics from one normalized event
python3 test/test_cowrie_security_alert.py
python3 test/test_react_dashboard_bridge.py
python3 test/test_react_counter_attack_executor.py
```

### Step 3: Generate Attacks (Optional)

**Option A: Wait for Real Attacks** (Recommended)
- Cowrie will automatically capture attacks from the internet
- Attacks usually start within minutes to hours
- View logs: `tail -f cowrie-logs/cowrie.json | jq`

**Option B: Simulate Attacks**
```bash
# Try to SSH into the honeypot (will be logged as attack)
ssh root@localhost -p 2222
# Try common passwords: root, admin, password, 123456
```

**Option C: Run Demo with Sample Data**
```bash
flink-cowrie demo cowrie
```

